nodecode-memlock is a rewrite of Sahil-SS9's Sahil-SS9/hermes-memlock:

  https://github.com/Sahil-SS9/hermes-memlock
  commit 123b9fd252d6fc2c9c7c4f254164915874aa54d5
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Sahil Saghir (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's core promise, kept in the same words -- a pin
is one standing instruction the operator wants to outlive the conversation, it
carries a text, a one-line reminder and a priority, and it comes back in front
of the model when the conversation no longer carries it. The guard_pin tool
becomes the pin tool with the same pin/update/unpin/list actions over the same
fields; /guard becomes /pins; the durable pin file and its JSON shape are the
rewrite's own, one file with a pins array instead of one file per pin plus a
manifest. The pinned text is flattened to one line and the reminder trimmed
to its first sentence, as the original did, so a pin cannot spoof a second
reminder item.

What was left out, and why: the original watched the host's conversation for
a compaction marker, audited each anchor with keyword probes (or sentence
embeddings) and re-injected only the anchors that had drifted out of the live
region. This image folds, thins and edits the conversation itself, so the pins
ride the first request of every turn instead: always in front of the model,
never aged out, no marker to detect and no audit to configure -- the drift
thresholds, probe lists, semantic detection and the sentence-transformers
dependency all go. Also left out: per-session pin scope (a pin here is the
organism's, read by every session, which is the original's global scope made
the default), pin version history and rollback, the alert script and
integrity score, the static anchor tier read from config, the reverse-audit
preference adapters (Severian, Mnemosyne, an MCP command), the setup wizard
and the Claude Code shim.
